data.content:
**No Thread.Abort migration is needed here.**

Your code only uses `Thread.Start()`, `Thread.Join()`, and `Thread.Sleep()` — none of these are affected by the Thread.Abort breaking change. They work identically on .NET 8. The code will compile and run correctly as-is on `net8.0`.

---

**Optional modernization** (unrelated to Thread.Abort migration):

If you'd like to take advantage of modern .NET, you could replace the manual thread management with `Parallel.ForEach` or `Task.Run`:

```csharp
public class SimpleWorker
{
    public void RunBatch(List<WorkItem> items)
    {
        Parallel.ForEach(items, item => Process(item));
    }

    private void Process(WorkItem item)
    {
        Thread.Sleep(100);
        item.Result = ComputeResult(item.Input);
    }
}
```

This is purely optional — it gives you automatic thread-pool management and better scalability, but the original code is fully functional on .NET 8 without changes.
